item.text:
Reused evidence: none beyond the local prior.

New evidence:
1. The State Department report gives the broad 2022 baseline: unlawful killings, disappearances, torture, arbitrary arrest, media restrictions, and weak accountability.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Nigeria","publisher":"USDOS / ecoi.net","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089140.html","claim":"The report lists credible reports of unlawful and arbitrary killings, forced disappearances, torture by government, arbitrary arrest, serious restrictions on expression and media, and weak accountability for major abuses.","locator":"Executive summary, line 41","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official US government human-rights report summarizing the year's record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers events of 2022 and the year-end rights picture","ruler_attribution":"State-level record under Buhari's administration; not proof of his personal order, but directly relevant to ruler responsibility.","contrary_evidence":["The summary is broad and does not isolate Buhari's direct command in each case.","It aggregates multiple issue areas rather than a single incident."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.5","3B.6","3B.8","3B.10"]}

2. The same report says security services used torture and inhuman treatment, and Amnesty documented 62 cases of arbitrary arrest, ill-treatment, and torture.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Nigeria","publisher":"USDOS / ecoi.net","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089140.html","claim":"Security services reportedly committed torture and inhuman treatment of suspects, detainees, and prisoners, and Amnesty documented 62 cases of arbitrary arrest, ill-treatment, and torture in southeastern states.","locator":"Lines 71-72","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Directly quoted government report with a cited NGO corroboration inside it.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year conduct and immediate reporting window","ruler_attribution":"Not uniquely Buhari-personal, but squarely within the federal security apparatus he headed.","contrary_evidence":["The cited Amnesty material is location-specific rather than nationwide.","The report does not show which abuses had direct presidential awareness."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.5"]}

3. The same report says the government limited peaceful assembly and sometimes used excessive force against protesters, including an Osogbo protest where police allegedly opened fire and no investigation followed.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Nigeria","publisher":"USDOS / ecoi.net","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089140.html","claim":"The government limited peaceful assembly, security services sometimes used excessive force to disperse protesters, and an Osogbo protest ended with police allegedly opening fire and no investigation.","locator":"Lines 200-207","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official human-rights report with a specific 2022 protest incident and government response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year conduct","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the state security system under Buhari's presidency, though the specific shooting is a local incident.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes police often started with tear gas before escalating.","This is not a presidential speech or direct order."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

4. The same report records repeated intimidation of journalists, arrests tied to criticism or sensitive reporting, and the continuation of Twitter-related restrictions and media penalties.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 Country Report on Human Rights Practices: Nigeria","publisher":"USDOS / ecoi.net","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2089140.html","claim":"Security services detained and harassed journalists, the NBC fined stations over bandit documentaries, and the Twitter suspension was only lifted after negotiations while media groups reported self-censorship and intimidation.","locator":"Lines 176-185 and 196-197","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official report with specific incidents and institutional actions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year conduct and immediate 2022 context","ruler_attribution":"State-level media climate under Buhari's administration; not evidence of a single personal order in each case.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes Twitter resumed in January 2022, so the ban was not continuous through the whole year.","Some actions were regulatory rather than overtly violent."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

5. HRW's 2022 Nigeria chapter captures the year's major insecurity shocks and says security forces and vigilantes were also implicated in abuses.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Nigeria","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/nigeria","claim":"HRW says 2022 featured the Owo church killings, the Kuje prison attack, and the Abuja-Kaduna train attack, while bandits carried out kidnappings, killings, rape, and looting and security forces and vigilantes were implicated in abuses.","locator":"Lines 51-56, 71-79","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Major independent rights synthesis with named 2022 incidents and pattern-level assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about events of 2022","ruler_attribution":"The attacks themselves were not ordered by Buhari, but the failures and abuse environment occurred under his government.","contrary_evidence":["This merges non-state violence with state abuse, so it is not a clean ruler-action metric.","It does not by itself prove direct presidential command in each incident."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.6","3B.7","3B.10"]}

6. HRW also says the government made no effort to ensure justice for the 2020 Lekki killings, while the ECOWAS court found the Twitter ban violated Nigerians' rights.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Nigeria","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/nigeria","claim":"HRW says authorities made no effort to ensure justice for the 2020 Lekki Toll Gate killings, though some victims later received compensation, and it notes the ECOWAS Court found the Twitter ban violated Nigerians' rights.","locator":"Lines 88-94","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent rights assessment citing both accountability failure and a partial remedy.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 consequences and year-end accountability state","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Buhari-era state, not to Buhari alone, but directly relevant to his responsibility for remedy and restraint.","contrary_evidence":["Some compensation was paid, so the picture is not total non-remedy.","The Lekki killings date from 2020, so this is about 2022 accountability rather than a new 2022 massacre."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.8","3B.10"]}

7. HRW's children and vulnerable-groups section shows continuing child abductions, military detention of children, and a 2022 UN handover protocol.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Nigeria","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/nigeria","claim":"HRW cites a UN report saying Boko Haram affiliates abducted and recruited children, including girls, and notes that over 45 children alleged to be associated with armed groups were detained by the military; Nigeria also signed a UN handover protocol in September 2022.","locator":"Lines 104-107","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent synthesis with a specific 2022 protective measure and a child-detention problem.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mixed inherited baseline and 2022 action","ruler_attribution":"The abuses are mostly inherited/non-state or military-conflict context; the handover protocol is a state remedial action under Buhari's government.","contrary_evidence":["The underlying child-abduction data are from 2021, so this is partly inherited baseline rather than new 2022 conduct.","The handover protocol is protective but limited in scope."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.4","3B.10"]}

8. Amnesty says many #EndSARS protesters were still detained without trial in 2022 and that panels had failed to deliver justice.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria: Two years on, more than 40 #EndSARS protesters still languishing in jail","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-10-20","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/10/nigeria-two-years-on-more-than-40-endsars-protesters-still-languishing-in-jail/","claim":"Amnesty said the vast majority of arrested #EndSARS protesters were still arbitrarily detained without trial, and that inquiry panels had failed to deliver justice for police brutality victims.","locator":"Lines 61-92","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights investigation with named detainee examples and accountability criticism.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about the 2022 detention and accountability state","ruler_attribution":"Federal-state police and justice system under Buhari's administration; not evidence of a single presidential order but of tolerated impunity.","contrary_evidence":["A few detainees were released in 2022, so the record is not total stasis.","This concerns the afterlife of 2020 protests, not a fresh 2022 crackdown."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.4","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

9. Buhari's Sokoto response is the clearest favorable 2022 ruler statement against mob violence and in favor of investigation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Reacts to Sokoto Killing Linked to Alledged Blasphemy, Says Violence Does Not Solve Problems","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-13","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-reacts-to-sokoto-killing-linked-to-alledged-blasphemy-says-violence-does-not-solve-problems/","claim":"Buhari condemned the mob killing of Deborah Samuel, demanded an impartial probe, and said no person has the right to take the law into their own hands.","locator":"Lines 27-36","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential statement on a dated State House page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year incident response","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Buhari as president and a clear statement of restraint and anti-incitement.","contrary_evidence":["This is a response after the killing, not proof of prevention.","A probe demand does not prove the probe happened effectively."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.9"]}

10. On the Kaduna train kidnappings, Buhari explicitly rejected lethal force because he could not guarantee low collateral damage and prioritized the safe release of hostages.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Assures that Government will do Its Best to Secure Release of Remaining Abductees","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-08-11","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-assures-that-government-will-do-its-best-to-secure-release-of-remaining-abductees/","claim":"Buhari said lethal military force was considered for the Kaduna train hostages but was reluctantly discarded because collateral damage could not be assured, and he said his primary concern was to get everyone released safe and unhurt.","locator":"Lines 27-40","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential explanation of a domestic security choice with explicit restraint reasoning.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 crisis response","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Buhari and strongly relevant to restraint under pressure.","contrary_evidence":["The need to choose among bad options reflects a severe security failure.","The hostages remained captive for months, so restraint did not equal immediate success."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.9","3B.10"]}

11. Buhari also condemned southeast killings and ordered a speedy investigation, which is another favorable but limited response to domestic political violence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Calls South East Killings Sad And Unwelcome","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-08-06","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-calls-south-east-killings-sad-and-unwelcome/","claim":"Buhari condemned attacks on non-indigenes and law enforcement officials in the southeast, promised a speedy investigation, and said the killings were sad and unwelcome.","locator":"Lines 27-33","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential statement on a dated State House page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year response","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Buhari as president.","contrary_evidence":["Condemnation is not the same as prevention or successful prosecution.","The statement does not show whether investigators achieved results."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.7","3B.9"]}

12. In his 1 October 2022 Independence Day address, Buhari called for peaceful disagreement and warned against hate speech and electoral violence.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Independence Day Address By President Buhari On Nigeria's 62nd Independence Anniversary Celebration","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-10-01","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/independence-day-address-by-president-buhari-on-nigerias-62nd-indepedence-anniversary-celebration/","claim":"Buhari urged Nigerians to settle grievances peacefully, called on aspirants to avoid hate speech and divisive tendencies, and said violence generally mars elections.","locator":"Lines 76-82","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential speech with explicit anti-incitement and anti-violence language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year speech","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Buhari, though it is rhetoric rather than an operational security order.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetoric alone does not show actual protection outcomes.","The address is self-presenting and may overstate government success."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

13. Buhari also told the armed forces to stay apolitical and improve human-rights compliance during the 2023 election period.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Canvasses Army Neutrality On 2023 Polls, Wants Human Rights Obligations Sustained","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-12-05","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-canvasses-army-neutrality-on-2023-polls-wants-human-rights-obligations-sustained/","claim":"Buhari directed the armed forces to remain apolitical, improve human-rights obligations, and support a peaceful environment for the 2023 general elections.","locator":"Lines 27-34","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential instruction on a dated State House page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 ruler conduct with direct domestic-safety relevance","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Buhari as commander-in-chief and relevant to civilian protection.","contrary_evidence":["This is forward-looking guidance, not a proven outcome.","The speech is aimed at elections rather than everyday policing."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

14. Reuters reported the Sokoto curfew after Deborah Samuel's killing, and Buhari's own response emphasized that violence and self-help were unacceptable.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria's Sokoto state declares curfew over student killing protests","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-05-15","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/05/15/us-nigeria-attacks","claim":"Reuters reported that Sokoto declared a 24-hour curfew after protests over Deborah Samuel's killing, with police firing tear gas and live bullets in the air; Buhari condemned the killing and called for an impartial investigation.","locator":"Article body, opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-syndicated contemporaneous event report with clear incident description and presidential reaction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year event and response","ruler_attribution":"The incident was local, but Buhari's condemnation is directly attributable and the state response reflects the domestic safety climate.","contrary_evidence":["The curfew response shows state containment, not solved underlying tensions.","The article is a short event report rather than a deep accountability investigation."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.9"]}

15. Reuters coverage of the Twitter agreement dispute shows rights groups feared Buhari's government was using the restoration terms to constrain legitimate speech.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rights group goes to court to get Nigeria to publish agreement with Twitter","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-02-13","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/02/13/us-nigeria-twitter","claim":"SERAP went to court to force publication of the Twitter agreement because it feared the terms could jeopardize freedom of expression, and the government had suspended Twitter after Buhari's tweet was deleted.","locator":"Lines 670-681","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters report on contemporaneous legal challenge and government position, with specific references to Buhari's tweet and the suspension.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year speech/restriction context","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Buhari's administration and information ministry, though not necessarily to Buhari's personal day-to-day direction.","contrary_evidence":["Twitter had been restored by the time of the report, so the ban was not continuously in force in 2022.","This is a legal dispute over speech controls, not a physical-violence incident."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

16. Reuters reported the Abuja-Kaduna train attack itself as a major lethal insecurity event.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Loud blast, gunshots as suspected bandits attack Nigerian train","publisher":"Reuters / MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/Loud-blast-gunshots-as-suspected-bandits-attack-Nigerian-train-39894008/","claim":"Reuters reported that at least seven people were killed and several were missing after suspected bandits attacked the Abuja-Kaduna passenger train, highlighting growing insecurity.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-syndicated contemporaneous event report with casualty detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year event","ruler_attribution":"The attack was not Buhari-ordered, but it was a direct domestic-safety failure under his administration.","contrary_evidence":["The attackers were non-state actors, so the event is not direct state violence.","The report does not establish the full chain of command for the response."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.7","3B.10"]}

17. Reuters' Owo report shows a mass-casualty civilian attack, including women and children, that defined the 2022 insecurity environment.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bereaved families face grief, trauma after Nigeria church attack","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/06/08/uk-nigeria-security","claim":"Reuters reported that the Owo church attack left at least 22 people dead and 50 injured, including civilians in a place of worship.","locator":"Opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-syndicated contemporaneous report with casualty figures and civilian setting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact target-year event","ruler_attribution":"The attackers were non-state actors, but the event is highly relevant to Buhari-era domestic protection failures.","contrary_evidence":["This is not a state action and therefore cannot by itself prove ruler culpability.","It mainly establishes the scale of the protection failure rather than the policy chain."],"lenses":["3B.6","3B.7","3B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I considered roughly 30 documents across State House pages, the State Department report, HRW, Amnesty, Reuters-syndicated coverage, Reuters Institute material, and a few secondary or retrospective articles.
- I retained the most useful non-duplicative evidence that directly bears on physical safety, state restraint, protest handling, accountability, and vulnerable-group protection.
- I rejected several documents as duplicate coverage of the same incidents, especially multiple versions of the Sokoto, train, and Twitter stories.
- I rejected a lot of 2021-only #EndSARS background because it was still useful context but not needed once the 2022 Amnesty and State Department material was kept.
- I rejected 2023-2026 articles about post-Buhari developments, because they were out of period or added no 2022-specific evidence.
- I also dropped broad economic or general political articles that did not connect to physical safety, restraint, or abuse.
- I did not re-fetch the local structured datasets you supplied; I treated them as the baseline and only added narrative, ruler-attributable evidence.

Remaining gaps:
- I still do not have a clean, direct documentary chain from Buhari to specific police or military abuses beyond broad command responsibility and public responses.
- The record on independent investigations and prosecutions for 2022 abuses remains incomplete, especially for the Osogbo shooting, the Kuje failure, and some south-east violence.
- I do not have a good 2022 source on how much Buhari personally oversaw or corrected state-sponsored vigilante activity.
- The evidence on women, children, and LGBTQI+ protection is real but still partly baseline and rights-report driven rather than ruler-action specific.
- More search could help on formal investigation outcomes, but it is unlikely to change the overall picture: recurring insecurity, notable abuse/impunity, and some but limited restraint and remedial rhetoric from Buhari.
